ir3: moving a shift inside an add changes the number when the constant is
negative.

create_shift() in ir3_nir_lower_io_offsets.c turns the byte offset of an SSBO
access into an element offset. When the offset has the shape `iadd constant,
foo` it rewrites `(C + x) >> s` as `(x >> s) + (C >> s)`, so that
nir_opt_offsets can fold the constant into the instruction's immediate field.

That identity does not hold for a negative constant. `buf[i - 1].field` is
ordinary code, and LLVM hands it over as `iadd 0xfffffffc, i*24` -- a
subtraction. `(i*24 - 4) >> 2` borrows from the bits above the two it drops;
`(i*24 >> 2) + (0xfffffffc >> 2)` adds 0x3fffffff instead of 0xffffffff and
does not. The two differ by 2^30, which puts the element offset a gigabyte past
the end of the buffer, and an out-of-range `ldib` returns zero without saying
so. It also drops the low bits of a constant that is not a whole number of
elements, which is the same mistake in a smaller way.

Measured on the phone's Adreno 7c+ Gen 3. `fbs_bistochastize_iter` of
PortraitFilters.metallib reads `grid_buf[id - 1].n` through exactly this shape
and sums `id + 1` terms when the read returns 0 instead of `id`. With input
built from the module's own metadata (tools/air/kinput.py, docs/air.md section
5) its four outputs should all be 1/sqrt(3) -- one term each -- and on the
device they were:

      word     device        1/sqrt(n)     Apple's own LLVM on the host
       0    0x3f13cd3a       1/sqrt(3)     0x3f13cd3a
       1    0x3f000000       1/sqrt(4)     0x3f13cd3a
       2    0x3ee4f92e       1/sqrt(5)     0x3f13cd3a
       3    0x3ed105ec       1/sqrt(6)     0x3f13cd3a

1/sqrt(3+i) in word i is the signature of the read returning 0 for every
thread, and 0 is what that address returns. The three fbs_* kernels of the
corpus carry this shape; the eight MetalFaceMask_* ones do not.

So split the constant out only when it survives being shifted and cannot be a
subtraction. Everything else keeps the shift outside the add, which is what the
code did before the folding and is always right.

--- a/src/freedreno/ir3/ir3_nir_lower_io_offsets.c
+++ b/src/freedreno/ir3/ir3_nir_lower_io_offsets.c
@@ -126,6 +126,32 @@
    return new_offset;
 }
 
+/* Whether `(c + x) >> shift` and `(x >> shift) + (c >> shift)` are the same
+ * number for every x, which is what moving the shift inside the add assumes.
+ *
+ * They are not, twice over. A constant whose low `shift` bits are set loses
+ * them to `c >> shift`, so the offset comes out short. Worse, a negative
+ * constant -- a struct field reached through `buf[i - 1]`, which is ordinary
+ * code -- is a subtraction, and `(x - 4) >> 2` borrows from the bits above
+ * while `(x >> 2) + (0xfffffffc >> 2)` adds 0x3fffffff and does not: the two
+ * differ by 2^30 and the SSBO read lands outside the buffer, where a6xx
+ * returns zero without saying so.
+ *
+ * That was measured: fbs_bistochastize_iter reads grid_buf[id - 1].n with
+ * exactly this shape, and on an Adreno 7c+ Gen 3 it read 0 for every thread,
+ * which turned 1/sqrt(3) in all four of its outputs into 1/sqrt(3), 1/sqrt(4),
+ * 1/sqrt(5), 1/sqrt(6) (docs/air.md section 5).
+ *
+ * So split only a constant that is a whole number of elements and cannot be a
+ * subtraction. Everything else keeps the shift outside the add, which is what
+ * the code did before the folding was added and is always right.
+ */
+static bool
+shift_splits_over_iadd(uint32_t c, int shift)
+{
+   return (c & BITFIELD_MASK(shift)) == 0 && c < (1u << 31);
+}
+
 static nir_def *
 create_shift(nir_builder *b, nir_def *offset, int shift)
 {
@@ -138,7 +164,9 @@
       nir_alu_instr *offset_instr = nir_instr_as_alu(offset->parent_instr);
 
       if (offset_instr->op == nir_op_iadd &&
-          nir_src_is_const(offset_instr->src[0].src)) {
+          nir_src_is_const(offset_instr->src[0].src) &&
+          shift_splits_over_iadd(
+             nir_src_as_const_value(offset_instr->src[0].src)->u32, shift)) {
          nir_def *new_shift = ir3_nir_try_propagate_bit_shift(
             b, offset_instr->src[1].src.ssa, -shift);
 
